Decision: if the threads tool is unavailable, we cannot honor AC3's machine-census. But the gh-807 gate + consumption-evidence + head-advanced checks already bound the blast radius. The rule query (threadsResolved:false) guarantees SOME unresolved threads. Options: (a) skip (fail closed) — sweep never fires on runtimes lacking the tool; (b) proceed with unresolved=1. The ticket's AC3 is about human-thread-only PRs — those never have consumption markers in the normal flow... actually they could: a manual agent:rework label on a machine PR with human threads → consumed → sweep would restore → rework leg runs → rework leg must not resolve human threads (gh-744 lives in the leg). Restoring is still safe-ish but noisy. I'll go fail-closed on tool-missing (log + return false) — "no census, no restore"; document. Hmm, but then in unit tests I must mock the tool. Fine — extraMocks supports it. But wait: is `github_get_pr_review_threads` a real bridge tool available under JSRunner? smProvider uses it — and smProvider is used by machineSmAgent (JSRunner) and githubSource (also JSRunner via smAsync). So yes, it exists in the runtime. Fail-closed on absence is right.

Tool result shape: smProvider reviewThreads parses `res.data.repository.pullRequest.reviewThreads.nodes` — nodes have isResolved and comments.nodes[0].author.login presumably. Let me check smProvider.reviewThreadList lines 588-620 for the exact node shape to count unresolved machine threads.

